WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:04.959
Hearing Oliver's shouts, Harry Maylie and Mr. Giles rushed outside.

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.134
They found Oliver, pale and agitated, pointing toward the meadows behind the house.

3
00:00:10.135 --> 00:00:13.634
"Fagin! Fagin!" cried Oliver.

4
00:00:13.635 --> 00:00:21.443
Mr. Giles had no idea what this name meant. But Harry had heard Oliver's story from his mother, so he knew who Fagin was.

5
00:00:21.444 --> 00:00:25.348
"Where did he go?" Harry picked up a large stick.

6
00:00:25.349 --> 00:00:29.856
"There!" Oliver pointed in the direction that Fagin had taken.

7
00:00:29.857 --> 00:00:38.015
"Then he must be in the ditch! Follow me!" Harry sprang over a hedge, with Mr. Giles and Oliver trying to keep up with him.

8
00:00:38.016 --> 00:00:42.356
Dr. Losberne, who was just returning from a walk, joined the chase.

9
00:00:42.357 --> 00:00:48.673
At last they reached the ditch, but no one was hiding in it. They walked to the top of a little hill.

10
00:00:48.674 --> 00:00:53.665
From there they could see all the open fields for several miles around.

11
00:00:53.666 --> 00:01:00.919
The village lay in one direction and the woods in another. But Fagin could not have reached either place without their spotting him.

12
00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:04.260
"It must have been a dream, Oliver," said Harry.

13
00:01:04.261 --> 00:01:15.571
"Oh no, sir." Oliver shuddered at the memory of Fagin's face. "I saw him too plainly for that. I saw them both as plainly as I see you now."

14
00:01:15.572 --> 00:01:18.228
"Both?" said Dr. Losberne.

15
00:01:18.229 --> 00:01:21.236
"Who was the other person?" asked Harry.

16
00:01:21.237 --> 00:01:25.903
"A strange man who shouted at me the day I took Mrs. Maylie's letter to the inn."

17
00:01:25.904 --> 00:01:29.213
"Are you sure they went this way?" Harry demanded.

18
00:01:29.214 --> 00:01:37.054
Oliver insisted, and the others wanted to believe him. But there were no footprints or any other sign of the two men.

19
00:01:37.055 --> 00:01:42.800
Harry sent Mr. Giles to ask about the two men at the village. No one had seen them.

20
00:01:42.801 --> 00:01:48.443
The next day Oliver and Harry went to the inn at the market town, hoping to hear something about the men there.

21
00:01:48.444 --> 00:01:54.221
This effort was equally fruitless, and in a few days, everyone forgot about the whole affair.

22
00:01:54.222 --> 00:02:00.221
Meanwhile Rose continued to recover. She left her room and was able to go outdoors.

23
00:02:00.222 --> 00:02:08.356
Cheerful voices and merry laughter could once again be heard in the cottage. But Oliver could not help noticing that something was wrong.

24
00:02:08.357 --> 00:02:15.689
Mrs. Maylie and her son were often closeted together for a long time. More than once Rose appeared to have been crying.

25
00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:24.427
At last one morning when Rose was alone in the breakfast room, Harry begged permission to speak to her. Rose turned pale and nodded.

26
00:02:24.428 --> 00:02:33.419
"I was brought here by the fear of losing you." He grasped her hand. "I dreaded that you would die and never know how devotedly I loved you."

27
00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.935
Tears came to Rose's eyes.

28
00:02:35.936 --> 00:02:46.594
"My own dear Rose!" Harry went on. "For years I've loved you. I hoped to win my way to fame and then come home and tell you I'd done it only for you.

29
00:02:46.595 --> 00:02:54.411
Alas, I am not famous. But I offer you my heart, which you have long possessed. Will you marry me?"

30
00:02:54.412 --> 00:03:02.721
"You have been ever so kind." Rose struggled to hold back her tears. "But I can only be your friend and not your wife."

31
00:03:02.722 --> 00:03:08.546
Harry still held Rose's hand. She covered her face with her other hand and wept.

32
00:03:08.547 --> 00:03:12.808
"And what are your reasons for this decision?" he asked in a low voice.

33
00:03:12.809 --> 00:03:16.713
"There is a stain upon my name," she said.

34
00:03:16.714 --> 00:03:21.792
"I cannot bring disgrace to you or to your mother, who has been like a mother to me.

35
00:03:21.793 --> 00:03:27.221
I won't have people saying that I married you to enhance my name and raise my position in the world."

36
00:03:27.222 --> 00:03:31.539
"If I'd been poor or sick, would you have turned me down?" asked Harry.

37
00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:36.038
Rose did not want to answer this question, but Harry pressed her to reply.

38
00:03:36.039 --> 00:03:41.031
"If you'd been even a little above me and I could have helped you, then we could have been happy.

39
00:03:41.032 --> 00:03:47.682
But now I'd only ruin your chances among ambitious and distinguished people. Farewell, Harry."

40
00:03:47.683 --> 00:03:50.269
Rose withdrew her hand from his.

41
00:03:50.270 --> 00:04:01.102
"Oh, Rose!" Harry cried. "Promise me one thing. Permit me to speak to you again on this subject in a year. And then I will never mention it again."

42
00:04:01.103 --> 00:04:08.507
"I'll promise you that," Rose said sadly, "although it will be useless to try to persuade me to marry you."

43
00:04:08.508 --> 00:04:13.840
She held out her hand again, but Harry embraced her instead and then hurried from the room.

44
00:04:13.841 --> 00:04:18.094
The next day Harry joined Oliver and Dr. Losberne at the breakfast table.

45
00:04:18.095 --> 00:04:22.411
"So you're going to be my traveling companion today," the doctor said to Harry.

46
00:04:22.412 --> 00:04:26.515
"Yesterday you were going to stay here and accompany your mother to the seaside.

47
00:04:26.516 --> 00:04:33.737
Then last night you urged, with great mystery, that we start out this morning before the ladies arise. What's going on?"

48
00:04:33.738 --> 00:04:38.237
"I simply changed my mind." Harry studied his tea.

49
00:04:38.238 --> 00:04:43.650
"Why? Have you heard something from those who have been encouraging you to run for Parliament?"

50
00:04:43.651 --> 00:04:48.062
"No. It's unlikely I'd hear anything at this time of year."

51
00:04:48.063 --> 00:04:51.983
"Well, you're an odd fellow," said the doctor.

52
00:04:51.984 --> 00:04:56.642
"But I know there are people working to get you into Parliament in the election before Christmas."

53
00:04:56.643 --> 00:05:00.793
"We shall see," Harry mumbled, not wanting to say anything more.

54
00:05:00.794 --> 00:05:08.468
The coach pulled up to the door. Mr. Giles came in for the baggage, and the doctor bustled out to see that it was loaded properly.

55
00:05:08.469 --> 00:05:13.468
"Oliver," said Harry in a low voice. "You can write well now?"

56
00:05:13.469 --> 00:05:17.618
Oliver looked up in surprise. "Yes, sir."

57
00:05:17.619 --> 00:05:24.690
"I won't be home again for some time. I wish you'd write to me every other week. Will you do that?"

58
00:05:24.691 --> 00:05:28.944
"Oh certainly, sir. I'll be proud to do it."

59
00:05:28.945 --> 00:05:35.603
"I'd like to know how my mother and Miss Rose are. You can tell me about the walks you take and what you talk about.

60
00:05:35.604 --> 00:05:41.690
And whether she—I mean they—seem happy and well. Do you understand?"

61
00:05:41.691 --> 00:05:44.428
"Oh yes," replied Oliver.

62
00:05:44.429 --> 00:05:54.007
"I'd rather you didn't mention this to the ladies because it might upset my mother," Harry said hurriedly. "Remember to tell me everything. I'm depending on you."

63
00:05:54.008 --> 00:05:57.349
Oliver was pleased and honored by his request.

64
00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:05.761
Harry went outside to join Dr. Losberne. As Harry jumped into the coach, he cast a last glance up at one of the cottage windows.

65
00:06:05.762 --> 00:06:09.642
Jingling and clattering, the coach pulled away in a cloud of dust.

66
00:06:09.643 --> 00:06:14.578
But there was one person whose eyes remained fixed on the spot where the coach had disappeared.

67
00:06:14.579 --> 00:06:26.111
"Harry seems happy," Rose said to herself as she sat at the upstairs window. "I'm very glad of that." But tears ran down her face.

